# Gap Analysis

## 1. Document Information

- **Project Name:** AI-Assisted Clinical Documentation Workflow Improvement
- **Document Type:** Gap Analysis
- **Version:** 1.0
- **Status:** Draft — Portfolio Project

## 2. Purpose

The purpose of this gap analysis is to compare the current manual clinical documentation workflow (As-Is) with the proposed AI-assisted workflow (To-Be), identify missing capabilities, and recommend actions to address the gaps.

## 3. Current Workflow (As-Is)

1. The doctor conducts the consultation.
2. The scribe prepares the clinical note.
3. The scribe corrects documentation mistakes.
4. The doctor reviews and checks the note.
5. The note is finalized in the EHR according to the existing workflow.

## 4. Proposed Workflow (To-Be)

1. The doctor conducts the consultation.
2. Authorized consultation information is provided to the AI-assisted system.
3. AI generates a draft clinical note.
4. The scribe reviews the draft and corrects documentation mistakes.
5. The doctor reviews the corrected note and checks clinical accuracy.
6. The doctor approves the note or requests further corrections.
7. The approved note is finalized or saved to the correct EHR record through an authorized workflow.

## 5. Gap Analysis Table

| ID | Area | As-Is Process | To-Be Process | Identified Gap |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| GAP-01 | Note preparation | Scribe prepares the note manually | AI generates a draft for the scribe | AI draft-generation capability is needed |
| GAP-02 | Information input | Information follows the existing documentation process | Authorized information is provided to the AI system | An approved input method must be defined |
| GAP-03 | Documentation structure | Note preparation follows the existing workflow | AI draft follows configured note sections | Standard note templates and section rules are needed |
| GAP-04 | Scribe corrections | Scribe corrects documentation mistakes | Scribe reviews and corrects the AI-generated draft | A review and editing interface is needed |
| GAP-05 | Doctor review | Doctor checks the prepared note | Doctor checks the corrected AI-assisted note | A clear review and approval workflow is needed |
| GAP-06 | Note status | Status handling follows the existing process | Draft, pending review, approved, and failure states are visible | Status tracking must be defined |
| GAP-07 | EHR saving | Note is finalized through the existing EHR workflow | Approved note is saved through an authorized workflow | EHR integration or an approved transfer method must be assessed |
| GAP-08 | Error handling | Handling depends on the current process | AI and saving failures are clearly communicated | Error handling and recovery procedures are needed |
| GAP-09 | Performance measurement | Baseline metrics may not be consistently available | Documentation performance is measured using agreed KPIs | Metric definitions and reporting are needed |
| GAP-10 | Privacy and security | Existing controls apply | AI-assisted processing must meet approved controls | Privacy, security, access, and audit requirements must be assessed |

## 6. Recommended Actions

| Gap ID | Recommended Action | Suggested Owner |
|---|---|---|
| GAP-01 | Evaluate and configure AI draft generation | AI / IT Team |
| GAP-02 | Define the approved consultation information input method | Clinical and Compliance Teams |
| GAP-03 | Define note templates and required sections | Clinical Stakeholders |
| GAP-04 | Define scribe review and correction functionality | Product / Development Team |
| GAP-05 | Define doctor review, approval, and return-for-correction steps | Clinical Stakeholders |
| GAP-06 | Define note statuses and status transitions | Business Analyst / Development Team |
| GAP-07 | Assess EHR integration and saving requirements | EHR / IT Team |
| GAP-08 | Define error messages and recovery procedures | Product / IT Team |
| GAP-09 | Establish baseline metrics and reporting rules | Business Analyst / Operations |
| GAP-10 | Complete privacy and security assessment | Compliance / Security Team |

## 7. Expected Benefits

The proposed workflow may:

- Reduce manual effort in preparing the initial note.
- Allow the scribe to focus on reviewing and correcting generated content.
- Preserve the doctor's role in checking clinical accuracy and approving the note.
- Improve visibility into the documentation process.
- Support measurement of workflow efficiency and documentation quality.

These are expected benefits and have not been measured in a live environment.

## 8. Risks and Considerations

- AI-generated content may be inaccurate or incomplete.
- The scribe and doctor may need training on the new workflow.
- EHR integration may require technical changes and approvals.
- Patient privacy and security requirements must be satisfied.
- The proposed workflow must be validated with stakeholders before implementation.

## 9. Conclusion

The main proposed change is the introduction of AI-assisted draft generation before the scribe's correction process. The scribe remains responsible for correcting documentation mistakes, and the doctor remains responsible for reviewing and approving the clinical note.

The identified gaps and recommended actions provide a starting point for requirements validation and future solution design.
